00:00:01,119 --> 00:00:08,480
Cowshed, and once on Midsummer Day, the anniversary of the rebellion.

2
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:16,119
Chapter 5 As winter drew on, Molly became more and more troublesome.

3
00:00:16,119 --> 00:00:20,599
She was late for work every morning and excused herself by saying that she had

4
00:00:20,599 --> 00:00:28,360
overslept, and she complained of mysterious pains, although her appetite was excellent.

5
00:00:28,360 --> 00:00:32,719
On every kind of pretext, she would run away from work and go to the drinking

6
00:00:32,719 --> 00:00:39,320
pool, where she would stand, foolishly gazing at her own reflection in the water.

7
00:00:39,320 --> 00:00:43,719
But there were also rumors of something more serious.

8
00:00:43,719 --> 00:00:49,480
One day, as Molly strolled blithely into the yard, flirting her long tail and

9
00:00:49,480 --> 00:00:53,519
chewing at a stalk of hay, Clover took her aside.

10
00:00:53,519 --> 00:00:57,719
>> Molly, I have something very serious to say to you.

11
00:00:57,719 --> 00:01:03,760
This morning, I saw you looking over the hedge that divides Animal Farm from Foxwood.

12
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:08,800
One of Mr. Pilkington's men was standing on the other side of the hedge, and I

13
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:13,320
was a long way away, but I am almost certain I saw this.

14
00:01:13,320 --> 00:01:18,480
He was talking to you, and you were allowing him to stroke your nose.

15
00:01:18,480 --> 00:01:21,760
What does that mean, Molly?

16
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:25,440
>> He didn't. I wasn't. It isn't true.

17
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:29,559
>> cried Molly, beginning to prance about and paw the ground.

18
00:01:29,559 --> 00:01:32,280
>> Molly, look me in the face.

19
00:01:32,280 --> 00:01:38,840
Do you give me your word of honor that that man was not stroking your nose?

20
00:01:38,840 --> 00:01:41,000
>> It isn't true.

21
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,719
>> repeated Molly, but she could not look Clover in the face, and the next moment,

22
00:01:45,719 --> 00:01:49,840
she took to her heels and galloped away into the field.

23
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:52,159
A thought struck Clover.

24
00:01:52,159 --> 00:01:56,239
Without saying anything to the others, she went to Molly's stall and turned

25
00:01:56,239 --> 00:01:59,039
over the straw with her hoof.

26
00:01:59,039 --> 00:02:04,480
Hidden under the straw was a little pile of lump sugar and several bunches of

27
00:02:04,480 --> 00:02:06,920
ribbon of different colors.

28
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:10,880
Three days later, Molly disappeared.

29
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:14,199
For some weeks, nothing was known of her whereabouts.

30
00:02:14,199 --> 00:02:21,679
Then, the pigeons reported that they had seen her on the other side of Willingdon.

31
00:02:21,679 --> 00:02:27,239
She was between the shafts of a smart dog cart painted red and black, which

32
00:02:27,239 --> 00:02:30,119
was standing outside a public house.

33
00:02:30,119 --> 00:02:36,320
A fat, red-faced man in check breeches and gaiters, who looked like a publican,

34
00:02:36,320 --> 00:02:40,320
was stroking her nose and feeding her with sugar.

35
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:46,719
Her coat was newly clipped, and she wore a scarlet ribbon round her forelock.

36
00:02:46,719 --> 00:02:49,000
She appeared to be enjoying herself, so
